docs/decisions/ADR-003-engine-orchestration.md:19:The CandidateSeed may originate from the Player, an observed LeelaAlien move, or an observed Boris-Trapsky move. Maia supplies modeled Opponent replies. Stockfish supplies Player-side continuations. A separately labeled AdversarialCheck may use Stockfish to look for concrete refutations but does not overwrite HumanPath.
docs/decisions/ADR-002-scenario-line-provenance.md:9:A mixed line may contain a Player idea, an actually observed external bot move, a Maia prediction, and a Stockfish continuation. Without provenance, users and AI agents can falsely describe a scenario as a bot's principal variation or mistake a human-likelihood prediction for best play.
docs/decisions/INDEX.md:7:| ADR-003 | Accepted | Use hybrid human-path scenario-line orchestration |
docs/decisions/ADR-001-human-in-the-loop.md:13:The Player supplies an assessment, plan, and candidate moves whenever practical. Chess Trainer may add candidate ideas and explain scenario lines, but the Player owns the Decision. Normal analysis has no external move-submission capability.
docs/decisions/ADR-001-human-in-the-loop.md:19:- Any future write integration requires a new ADR, separate capability specification, explicit confirmation, and audit trail.
docs/decisions/ADR-005-local-style-path.md:19:The CLI produces independent `StylePath` lines for Patricia, Jackal, and Seer. Stockfish 19 and Lichess external probes are not used by this mode. CSTal variants are deferred until a supported Wine or Linux runtime is configured.
docs/glossary.md:3:This glossary is mandatory vocabulary for Chess Trainer. It prevents common mistakes such as calling a mixed-source teaching line a principal variation, treating Maia as best defense, or attributing a Stockfish continuation to LeelaAlien.
docs/glossary.md:27:- a stable hash of the canonical position.
docs/glossary.md:37:A bounded conversation and computation around one `PositionSnapshot`. It contains Player reasoning, candidate seeds, requested horizon, generated lines, errors, and coaching review. It is not a game controller and does not submit moves.
docs/glossary.md:58:A strategic intention, not a forced line. Examples: attack the castled king, open the f-file, exchange into a favorable pawn ending, prevent a central break, improve the worst piece, or provoke a weakening move.
docs/glossary.md:74:A public Lichess bot commonly associated with unexpected, low-human-expectation move choices. Chess Trainer treats it as a source of practical candidate ideas only. The system must not claim LeelaAlien produced a line unless every move in that claimed line was actually observed from LeelaAlien in one game record.
docs/glossary.md:78:A public Lichess bot commonly associated with tricky and practical choices. Chess Trainer treats it as an optional source of one observed candidate idea, not as a full-line analysis provider.
docs/glossary.md:96:A human-like model trained to predict moves that players at a target strength are likely to make. In HumanPath mode, Maia supplies Opponent moves. Maia output must be labelled as modeled human-likelihood, not objective best play.
docs/glossary.md:108:A separate, explicitly labelled mode where Stockfish searches for strong defensive resources or tactical refutations. It answers "can this practical idea be punished?" It must not replace Maia plies in a HumanPath line.
docs/glossary.md:118:A `ScenarioLine` may be incomplete if a provider fails, a move is illegal, provenance is missing, or a terminal board state is reached.
docs/glossary.md:122:A line produced by one engine in an engine-analysis mode under declared settings. Do not use this term for default HumanPath output.
docs/glossary.md:155:A command that writes to an external chess platform, such as making a move, sending chat, accepting a challenge, resigning, or aborting. Platform commands are outside ordinary analysis and require their own future specification.
